{
 "id": "2026-11-23-a",
 "title": "Sugar makes kids hyper? Scientists tested it 🧁",
 "description": "Does sugar make kids hyper? In a 1994 double-blind study (New England Journal of Medicine), children lived on three diets for three weeks each: high in sugar, or sweetened with aspartame or saccharin. Nobody knew which diet was which. For the children their parents called sugar-sensitive, there was no difference on 39 behavior and thinking measures. Another 1994 study gave 35 boys a sugar-free drink and told half of their mothers it was a big dose of sugar. Those mothers rated their sons as more hyperactive and stayed physically closer to them. A 1995 JAMA meta-analysis of the research found that sugar does not affect children's behavior or thinking. The likely real culprits: exciting parties and our own expectations. (Sugar still matters for teeth and health; this is how the mind works, not medical advice.)\n\nSources: Wolraich et al., NEJM 1994; Hoover & Milich, Journal of Abnormal Child Psychology 1994; Wolraich, Wilson & White, JAMA 1995.",
